title: GUI asset endpoints
tags: [[Writing a panel type]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The open routes of the GUI's server that serve code and pages — panel files, the shell's JavaScript modules, the rendered documentation.

|!Route |!Serves |
|<<cmd "GET /panel/:name/*path">> |a file of the panel type `name`'s directory (`~/.config/metafolder/gui/panel-types/<name>/`), verbatim, with a type from its extension |
|<<cmd "GET /__*.js">> |the shell's helper modules, compiled into the GUI binary (`/__ui.js`, `/__menu.js`…) |
|<<cmd "GET /__commands/:generation/:file">> |the command modules `commands.js` and `user-commands.js` of `~/.config/metafolder/gui/`, side by side so that the second can import the first; `generation` is the shell's load count, ignored by the server, so a reload imports both afresh ([[User commands]]) |
|<<cmd "GET /docs/*path">> |a page of the rendered documentation (`~/.config/metafolder/docs/`), which the [[help panel]] reads |

They are the routes ''without'' the session token: they are loaded by `import()` or
`<link>`, which cannot send a header, and they hold shipped or user-written code, no
private data ([[Session tokens]]). The CORS policy is permissive for the same reason —
panels run in the shell's origin and fetch from this one.

A file path is taken as plain name components only — no `..`, no absolute segment —
so nothing outside the directory is reachable; anything unreadable is a `404`, a
a missing command module included. A panel file is served as it is on disk: an edit shows
at the next GUI restart, the module URL being cache-busted per session ([[Panel pitfalls]]).
The helper modules are compiled in, so editing one needs a rebuild of the GUI
([[Building]]).
